  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Sun Jul 19 2026\n\nThe Quad State region will sit on the inflection point between \ntroughing to the east and ridging to the west (across the Rockies). \nThis will allow for northwest flow into the region aloft. Shower and \nthunderstorm activity will develop each afternoon during peak \nheating. The convection will be a bit more focused this afternoon \nand evening as a frontal remains nearly stationary across the \nregion. The main concern this afternoon will be the very heavy \nrainfall rates on already saturated soils. PWAT values are on the \norder of 2-2.4 inches this afternoon with dewpoints pushing near \n80degrees, again pointing at that heavy rainfall/flooding potential. \nThe lack of wind shear and the more widespread nature of the \nconvection should limit any severe potential through this evening. \n\nA much stronger cold front will sag south through the area on \nTuesday, bringing storms, but some relief from the heat/humidity. \nInstability is forecast to be 2000-3000 J/kg with deep layer shear \nincreasing to around 20-30 kts, especially across southern IL into \nsouthwest IN. The combination of increased wind speeds aloft and the \ninstability, will lead to another round of strong to severe storms. \nCurrently, the greatest wind shear is expected to remain to the \nnortheast of the area, but it will definitely be worth keeping an \neye on over the next few days. In fact, southwest IN is already \nhighlighted with a slight risk of severe weather on Tuesday with a \nmarginal draped to the southwest of that area. At an early glance, \ndamaging winds and large hail would be the main threats.\n\nOutside of any thunderstorm activity, heat index values are expected \nto be in the 100-105 degree range through at least Tuesday as \ntemperatures warm into the low 90s and dewpoints run in the 70s. In \nfact, some locations could even see heat index values approach or \nexceed 110 degrees by Tuesday (the hottest day). This has lead to \nthe issuance of a Heat Advisory for much of the area through Tuesday.\n\nWildfire smoke from Canadian wildfires will likely become an issue \nat times through next week. In fact, it may become more widespread \nacross the Quad State toward the middle to end of next week with a \nmore northerly component to the wind aloft.",
